A||||1||Roy||Dropping the craddle||||1||
Z||000000||Roy||05-18-2000||03:17 PM||||Can anyone link me to a webpage that explains in depth how to drop the craddle. Ive pulled the engine out of the top on a 84, now I need to drop the craddle on a 88gt to replace the transaxle. And I figure Ill do all the seals on the engine while its out. Thanks in advance.||||reg||1
Z||000001||bHooper||05-18-2000||04:40 PM||||Having just done this, and with little mechanical background, I can assure you that it is not all that complicated.<P>Get yourself a pallet, 4' square or so, 3 or 4 2x4's and a 4x4, 8' long.  You will also need plenty of towels handy, as you will probably lose a fair amount of coolant out the pipes, even if you do drain it.  The 2x4's should be set along the length and width of the cradle to allow for jacking points.<P>The 4x4 needs to be placed under the body, about 12" forward of the front cradle mounts.  You will use this to jack up the car off of the engine.<P>It actually is rather simple, as long as you dont run into any trouble spots...i.e.: stuck bolts, ect.<P>As for mounting the tranny, George from the Fiero Shop gave me a excellent tip... get 2 long bolts (about 4-5"long) with the same thread pattern as the tranny to engine bolts.  Cut the heads off, thread them into the top 2 holes beneath the crossover (of course this is all after you have repalced the clutch and PP), hang the tranny by these.  Hook up a remote starter to the starter and fire away.  The starter will spin the flywheel and set the tranny!  I do suggest giving it a little push as it pulls.  But this method is so simple!<P>Now, I'm sure I missed some thing, and I skipped over most of the matter of fact stuff.  But this should get you started.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5007/fiero_osg/engine.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5007/fiero_osg/engine.html</A> <P>Hope this works,<BR>hoop||||reg||1
